.TH CONN 1 "" "conn 0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a station for one operator directing many hands on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
conn shows the processes running in each project, what each is doing, and which
one is waiting for the operator, and puts the operator in front of any of them.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBPURPOSE.\fR This manual describes conn and its operation.
.TP
1-2.
\fBSCOPE.\fR conn is a station for one operator directing many hands on one
machine. It shows the processes running in each project, what each is doing, and
which one is waiting for the operator, and it puts the operator in front of any
of them. It does not edit files, run builds, or hold conversations. The hands
do.
.TP
1-3.
\fBDEFINITIONS.\fR Terms used in this manual.
.RS
.TP
\fBOperator\fR
The person using conn.
.TP
\fBHand\fR
A process conn watches: a shell, an agent, or a command a shell is running. Also
called a station hand.
.TP
\fBAgent\fR
An AI coding assistant running in a terminal, such as Claude Code. An agent can
stop and wait for the operator to answer it.
.TP
\fBPlace\fR
The project directory a hand runs in: a git repository, or a folder that holds
several repositories.
.TP
\fBStanding\fR
The state conn reports for a hand: WORKING, ACTIVE, IDLE, or WAITING.
.TP
\fBWaiting\fR
The standing of an agent that has stopped and needs an answer from the operator,
such as a permission or a question.
.TP
\fBRail\fR
The left pane of the window, forty-four columns wide. The watch and the other
views are shown there.
.TP
\fBWatch\fR
The list of hands, grouped by place, in the order they started.
.TP
\fBSlot\fR
The right pane of the window. It shows one hand.
.TP
\fBBar\fR
The status line at the bottom of the window. It shows the state of the keys on
the left and one lamp per hand on the right.
.RE
.TP
1-4.
\fBINDICATIONS.\fR The station indicates two things: what each hand is doing,
and which hand is waiting. The watch and the lamps on the bar show the first.
The word WAITING on the watch, blinking, and a lamp on the bar in the same color
show the second; \fBtab\fR goes to it.
.TP
1-5.
\fBCOMMANDS.\fR \fBconn\fR alone brings up the console and continues to the
watch. Other commands are given by name after it. \fB\-\-light\fR and
\fB\-\-dark\fR set the ground, ahead of a command if one is given.
.PP
.I "SYNOPSIS"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
Brings up the console, then the watch.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Takes the server down with everything in it, and reports what went.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Writes conn's theme for Claude Code.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Writes conn's colorscheme for nvim.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Sets the ground light.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Sets the ground dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Prints this table.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Prints the build.
.RE
